{
  "image_name": "1ea3dd902b60c252792296c4b5c7c8df.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several bioelectronic strategies for controlling cell behavior and treating disease by using electrochemical reactions near living tissue. Across the panels, electrodes and encapsulated devices are shown generating local chemical cues rather than simply delivering current.\n\nOne concept is neuromodulation: a cathode coated with a nanocatalyst sits above a platinum anode in a physiological solution, where an applied current appears to reduce oxygen and consume protons, changing the local chemical environment around neurons. Another concept addresses oxygen supply in implanted cell therapies. A biohybrid implant contains therapeutic cell capsules inside an immunoisolating, elastomeric enclosure, with an electrochemical oxygenation module at the base that provides on-site oxygen because host tissue alone is insufficient. This supports cell survival and secretion of therapeutics.\n\nA third example shows electronic control of gene expression. Electrodes generate a mediator, apparently reactive oxygen species, which activates an intracellular KEAP1–NRF2 pathway and downstream transcription, illustrated here as leading to insulin production. The final panel translates this idea to diabetes management in a mouse, where a small implanted electrode setup powered by batteries stimulates engineered cells under the skin. Overall, the figure highlights electrochemical interfaces as tools to modulate physiology, sustain implants, and trigger therapeutic protein release.",
